WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.968
-Our first guest is an Emmy,
Grammy, and Tony winner

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:07.334
who wrote eight original songs
for the movie "Encanto,"

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:09.834
which is streaming now
on Disney+

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:12.467
and whose soundtrack is
the number one soundtrack

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:13.868
on the Billboard charts.

00:00:13.868 --> 00:00:17.801
Ladies and gentlemen, please
welcome Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.634
Yeah.
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:26.367
That's what I'm talking about,
you feel the love, right?

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:29.667
Do you hear that? Come on.
-Hi, everybody.

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:32.000
-Come on.
-Love you.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.400
-It is great to see you.
I love that.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.367
I love the short cut,
the short haircut.

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:41.400
-Thank you. Yeah. I left my hair
in four movies back in 2020.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.667
-You really did. Yeah.

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.868
I want to talk about a little
bit, if we can --

00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:46.567
Tick, Tick, Boom.

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:47.767
That was the last time you were
on our show

00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:49.367
right before that release.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.100
Were you happy
with the response?

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.801
I mean, it's been nominated
for all these awards.

00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:57.334
-I'm so thrilled.
It's it's just been amazing.

00:00:57.334 --> 00:01:01.267
The response to my artists
who are at a crossroads

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:02.901
in their lives
and see the movie,

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:05.133
and that's exactly
what it speaks to.

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:08.234
And then what I didn't
anticipate was folks

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.434
who aren't artists
who kind of understand it.

00:01:10.434 --> 00:01:12.634
Like I've, I've heard
from a lot of young writers

00:01:12.634 --> 00:01:14.934
who showed the movie
to their families to,

00:01:14.934 --> 00:01:17.667
like, make them understand
what they go through.

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:22.367
I remember screening a rough cut
of the movie from my in-laws

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:25.834
and my father in-law,
who is not in the arts at all,

00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:28.000
there's a scene in the movie
where, you know,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
the agent tells Jonathan
Jonathan's work falls through

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.734
and he goes,
"What am I supposed to do now?"

00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:36.100
And she says,
"You write the next one

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.767
and then you write the next one
and then you do that again."

00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:42.534
And that's what it is
to be a writer.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:45.701
And I remember my father in-law
like looking at me

00:01:45.701 --> 00:01:51.334
as if he saw me for the first
time and went, "Jesus Christ.

00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:53.467
Is that your life?"
-Yeah.

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:55.868
Is that right?
And congrats, by the way,

00:01:55.868 --> 00:01:59.300
if you just got nominated
for Best Director by the DGA.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.968
So that's.

00:02:02.968 --> 00:02:07.200
First jet, first jam out!
Come on, buddy.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.133
It's streaming now on Netflix
if you want to see.

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:12.834
Just, it's just a great movie,
great performances all around.

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:14.300
-And Andrew Garfield,
he's very good.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.167
Andrew Garfield is just an
insane --

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:17.834
did he, by the way,
did Andrew Garfield,

00:02:17.834 --> 00:02:21.100
did he tell you about
"Spider-Man" at all?

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.734
When you worked with him?
-He didn't.

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:24.601
But can I tell the story?

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:27.767
So the day it leaked,
he might be involved.

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:30.901
He was on my set.
We were still filming.

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:33.601
And between shots,
I kind of quietly went over

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:35.767
to him and was like,

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:37.667
"Are you in the new
'Spider-Man'?"

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:41.400
And he exact reaction was,
"What? Shut up, shut up.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.400
Ha ha ha, shut up."

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.934
And I walked away thinking, "Oh,
he's in the new 'Spider-Man.'"

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.400
And then when I saw him
on your show,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.734
like, he was a total
pro at denying it.

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:55.033
-Oh yeah.
-But he had to get there.

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.868
-Yeah, he had to get there,
that's right.

00:02:57.868 --> 00:03:01.534
Let's talk about "Encanto"
right now.

00:03:01.534 --> 00:03:02.801
Congrats on all this.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:07.067
It's streaming now on Disney+.

00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:08.868
You wrote eight
original songs for it,

00:03:08.868 --> 00:03:12.767
and the soundtrack is number one
on the Billboard charts.

00:03:12.634 --> 00:03:15.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:18.868
Bumped Adele out of
the number one spot, by the way.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:21.434
-I didn't my heart.
-Not it's not in your heart.

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:23.701
Me neither. Not my heart, yeah.
Of course love, Adele.

00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:27.267
We don't talk about Bruno
is officially the biggest single

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:30.033
ever from a Disney movie
bigger than "Let It Go."

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:33.133
Did you -- what?

00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:36.200
Did you know, did you feel
coming, did you see this coming?

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:41.167
-No. Mainly because
it's like group numbers

00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:44.634
never get the same kind
of love like that.

00:03:44.634 --> 00:03:48.834
The ballads get or the,
you know, the comedy songs get.

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:50.534
But I didn't know it was catchy

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:52.334
because when I was first
writing it,

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:54.400
I would catch my kids singing it

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.601
just from overhearing me
writing it.

00:03:56.601 --> 00:04:00.167
And I remember I had had to have
a like NDA

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:02.701
for toddlers conversation
with them.

00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:05.734
I was like, "You guys can't
sing this at school."

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:07.501
We can only sing this around
the house

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:11.067
because you know, your friends
don't don't know the song yet.

00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:13.667
-So, we don't talk about,
we don't. We don't talk.

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:15.767
-We don't talk about.
We don't talk about Bruno.

00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:17.467
-Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:20.567
All the songs
I love the beautiful song.

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:24.467
Is it Dos Oruguitas means
two caterpillars?

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:26.734
-Yeah.
-And it's just gorgeous.

00:04:26.734 --> 00:04:29.601
But is this the first song
you've written in completely

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:30.968
in Spanish?

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:32.167
Yeah, it was.
It was the first song

00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:33.934
that written beginning
to end in Spanish.

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:38.067
The second Colombia, Mi Encanto
is earlier in the movie,

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:39.567
but honestly,

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:42.701
it's the the moment
where that song happens

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:45.501
is this moment of kind of
unspeakable tragedy.

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:50.400
You know, Abuela is telling this
really tough story from her,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.167
from her youth and the notion
of her singing that felt wrong.

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:58.701
So it was like, "Alright, so we
need to have something that --

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:00.167
Sometimes you figure out
what a song

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:02.000
is by figuring out
what it's not.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.000
And I thought, "We need, like,
a nature metaphor."

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.167
We need it to feel like
a folk song

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:09.968
that's kind of always existed.
And so then I thought,

00:05:09.968 --> 00:05:13.067
"What if it's about
these two caterpillars in love

00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:14.901
and they don't want
to let go of each other,

00:05:14.901 --> 00:05:16.234
but they have to let
go of each other

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:17.934
so that the miracle can happen

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:22.667
and they can become
their next selves?"

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:24.400
-I mean, it's just

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.000
it's describing everyone's
welling up here.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.367
They are through Zoom.

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:30.667
You're the first person to make
someone cry through Zoom.

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:32.567
Oh my gosh.
-Well, you know, it's funny,

00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:34.300
and I kind of just
sort of pitch that,

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.634
and I wrote the first verse
and chorus

00:05:37.634 --> 00:05:39.934
and played it for the rest
of the creative team

00:05:39.934 --> 00:05:42.133
at our weekly music
Zoom meetings.

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:45.100
I only had like the first verse
through the chorus,

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.534
and I played it for them,

00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:48.200
and I just remember
that reaction.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.834
It was just like, "You should
probably keep writing it."

00:05:51.834 --> 00:05:53.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.868
I think we got something there.
Okay.

00:05:55.868 --> 00:05:59.000
Yeah, I want to show
everyone a clip.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.400
Here is that song
from Disney's "Encanto."

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.701
Take a look at this.

